# Display server (XLibre)
# Comes from the third-party XLibre repo configured in build.sh, not Devuan.
# 'xlibre' Conflicts with and Provides 'xorg', so this is a straight swap --
# nothing else in this list needs to know which X server it got.
xlibre
# Ships the repo's signing key as a dpkg-managed file, so the ISO keeps
# verifying XLibre updates after the key baked into the image is superseded.
xlibre-archive-keyring

# GPU / graphics drivers
# xlibre pulls the modesetting driver via xserver-xlibre-core, but the full
# video driver set is only a Recommends -- so install it explicitly since the
# build uses --no-install-recommends. Needed for virtio-gpu under UTM/QEMU as
# well as real hardware.
# Heads-up: this pulls xserver-xlibre-video-amdgpu, which needs mesa >= 25.2.6
# while excalibur ships 25.0.7. That is why XLibre upstream tells Excalibur
# users they HAVE to enable backports. Note that merely having backports in
# sources.list is not enough -- it is NotAutomatic, so build.sh also pins the
# mesa stack to it. Net effect: mesa is the 26.x backport image-wide, not stable.
xserver-xlibre-video-all
mesa-utils
mesa-vulkan-drivers
libva2
#x86_64 xserver-xlibre-video-intel
#x86_64 xserver-xlibre-video-amdgpu
#x86_64 intel-media-va-driver-non-free

# NVIDIA (x86_64 only)
# nvidia-driver is the non-free userspace -- GLX/EGL/Vulkan plus nvidia_drv.so --
# and it pulls nvidia-kernel-dkms, the proprietary kernel module, as the first
# branch of its kernel-module alternation. The open modules
# (nvidia-open-kernel-dkms) were considered and rejected for now: they need
# Turing (GTX 16xx / RTX 20xx) or newer, which would leave every GTX 900/1000
# card on the generic framebuffer. Both halves are equally non-free in userspace,
# so "open" would have bought licensing cleanliness only on the kernel side.
#
# Installing the package is not enough on its own -- the module still has to be
# BUILT for the kernel this image ships, and KMS still has to be switched on.
# Both happen in the "NVIDIA kernel module" step in build.sh; see the comment
# there for why neither can be done from this list.
#
# This works alongside XLibre because xserver-xlibre-core deliberately declares
# "Provides: xserver-xorg-core (= 2:21.98), xorg-video-abi-25", which satisfies
# xserver-xorg-video-nvidia's "xserver-xorg-core (<< 2:21.99)" dependency.
#x86_64 nvidia-driver
#x86_64 nvidia-kernel-dkms
# Build inputs for the DKMS module. linux-headers-amd64 tracks linux-image-amd64
# exactly (same source, same version), so the headers can never drift from the
# kernel in the squashfs. build.sh purges the headers again once the module is
# built; build-essential stays because Gershwin is compiled from source anyway.
#x86_64 linux-headers-amd64
#x86_64 build-essential
#x86_64 dkms

